<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The log in / register screen (AuthFragment).

    **One screen, two forms.** Both modes share the header, the "OR" divider, the Google button and the
    footer link, and differ only in the block of fields between. So both blocks are laid out here, one
    above the other, and AuthFragment shows one and hides the other (visibility GONE) according to the
    mode in AuthViewModel. Hiding rather than removing is what makes "switching preserves anything
    already typed" work for free: a GONE EditText keeps its text, and since every field has its own id,
    Android also saves and restores that text across rotation without any code.

    **How the user moves between the two.** A line at the foot of each form — "New to PetCare? Create
    an account", and its counterpart back — rather than the segmented toggle that used to sit above the
    fields. Log in is the screen almost everyone arriving here wants, and a toggle across the top gave
    registering equal billing at the point where a returning user's eye should be going straight to the
    email field. The link at the bottom is where someone who has *finished* reading the form and found
    nothing for them will look.

    **The locked panel.** After five wrong passwords for one address, the whole form is replaced by
    view_auth_locked.xml — see the note at the top of that file. Everything between the header and the
    end of the page is therefore wrapped in `authContent`, so the fragment can hide the form, the
    divider, the Google button and the footer in one move rather than six.

    **Why a ScrollView.** The register form is taller than most phones — the mockup itself is drawn in
    two halves — and the keyboard takes up to half the screen on top of that. MainActivity pads the
    window by the keyboard's height, and the ScrollView is what lets the column then scroll the focused
    field into view.

    **Why LinearLayout inside.** The page is a single column where each element simply sits below the
    last; the only side-by-side arrangements (the helper/link row, the divider, the consent row) are
    small horizontal LinearLayouts. A ConstraintLayout would express the same thing with a chain of
    constraints per view and gain nothing.
-->
<ScrollView xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.Page"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    tools:context=".ui.auth.AuthFragment">

    <LinearLayout
        style="@style/PetCare.Auth.Column"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content">

        <!-- Logo, name, tagline, and this mode's heading and subtitle. The heading and subtitle are
             hidden while the account is locked: the dark panel below carries its own, and two
             headings stacked would read as two screens at once. -->
        <include
            android:id="@+id/header"
            layout="@layout/view_auth_header" />

        <!-- Everything below the header, hidden as one block while sign-in is locked. -->
        <LinearLayout
            android:id="@+id/authContent"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/auth_header_gap"
            android:orientation="vertical">

            <!-- ============================ Log in fields ============================ -->

            <LinearLayout
                android:id="@+id/loginForm"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:orientation="vertical">

                <!-- labelFor ties the label to its field, so TalkBack reads "Email or phone" when the
                     field is focused, even though the field itself shows no floating label. -->
                <TextView
                    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.FieldLabel"
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:labelFor="@id/identifierInput"
                    android:text="@string/login_identifier_label" />

                <!-- Accepts either an email or a phone number, so the input type is plain text with
                     autofill hints for both — the keyboard must allow letters and "@".

                     AuthFragment also watches this field's text, which no other field on the screen
                     needs: the failed-attempt count and the lock belong to the address being typed,
                     so they have to follow it. See AuthViewModel.onIdentifierChanged. -->
                <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout
                    android:id="@+id/identifierLayout"
                    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.Field"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content">

                    <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputEditText
                        android:id="@+id/identifierInput"
                        style="@style/PetCare.Auth.FieldInput"
                        android:layout_width="match_parent"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                        android:autofillHints="emailAddress,phone"
                        android:hint="@string/login_identifier_hint"
                        android:imeOptions="actionNext"
                        android:inputType="textEmailAddress" />
                </com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout>

                <TextView
                    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.FieldLabel"
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:labelFor="@id/loginPasswordInput"
                    android:text="@string/login_password_label" />

                <!-- endIconMode="password_toggle" puts the eye icon inside the right edge of the
                     box. Material swaps the icon between "eye" and "eye with a stroke through it" as
                     the field is revealed and hidden, so the icon always shows the current state, and
                     each field keeps its own toggle — revealing one never reveals another.

                     Showing a password on request is not a weakening: it is the standard answer to
                     typos in a masked field, it is the user's own choice made on their own screen,
                     and the alternative — typing a long password blind — reliably produces *weaker*
                     passwords, because people pick ones that are easy to type.

                     actionDone on the keyboard submits the form, wired in AuthFragment. -->
                <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout
                    android:id="@+id/loginPasswordLayout"
                    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.Field"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    app:endIconContentDescription="@string/cd_toggle_password_visibility"
                    app:endIconMode="password_toggle">

                    <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputEditText
                        android:id="@+id/loginPasswordInput"
                        style="@style/PetCare.Auth.FieldInput"
                        android:layout_width="match_parent"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                        android:autofillHints="password"
                        android:imeOptions="actionDone"
                        android:inputType="textPassword" />
                </com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout>

                <TextView
                    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.Helper"
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/auth_label_gap"
                    android:text="@string/login_password_helper" />

                <!-- The wrong-password warning, between the helper line and the reset link exactly as
                     the mockups place it: directly under the field it is about, and directly above
                     the way out of the problem it describes. Starts GONE and takes no space until
                     AuthFragment has something to report. -->
                <include
                    android:id="@+id/loginAlert"
                    layout="@layout/view_auth_alert"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_sm"
                    android:visibility="gone" />

                <!-- layout_gravity="end" right-aligns the link within the column (and flips to the
                     left in right-to-left languages, which is why "end" rather than "right"). The
                     negative end margin cancels the button's own inner padding so its text lines up
                     with the fields' right edge. -->
                <com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton
                    android:id="@+id/forgotPasswordButton"
                    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.TextLink"
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_gravity="end"
                    android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_xs"
                    android:layout_marginEnd="-8dp"
                    android:text="@string/login_forgot_password" />

                <com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton
                    android:id="@+id/loginButton"
                    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.PrimaryButton"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_sm"
                    android:text="@string/login_submit" />
            </LinearLayout>

            <!-- ============================ Register fields ============================
                 Starts GONE; AuthFragment reveals it when the footer link switches modes. -->

            <LinearLayout
                android:id="@+id/registerForm"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:orientation="vertical"
                android:visibility="gone"
                tools:visibility="gone">

                <TextView
                    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.FieldLabel"
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:labelFor="@id/fullNameInput"
                    android:text="@string/register_full_name_label" />

                <!-- textPersonName|textCapWords capitalises each word as the name is typed. -->
                <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout
                    android:id="@+id/fullNameLayout"
                    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.Field"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content">

                    <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputEditText
                        android:id="@+id/fullNameInput"
                        style="@style/PetCare.Auth.FieldInput"
                        android:layout_width="match_parent"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                        android:autofillHints="name"
                        android:hint="@string/register_full_name_hint"
                        android:imeOptions="actionNext"
                        android:inputType="textPersonName|textCapWords" />
                </com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout>

                <TextView
                    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.FieldLabel"
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:labelFor="@id/emailInput"
                    android:text="@string/register_email_label" />

                <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout
                    android:id="@+id/emailLayout"
                    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.Field"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content">

                    <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputEditText
                        android:id="@+id/emailInput"
                        style="@style/PetCare.Auth.FieldInput"
                        android:layout_width="match_parent"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                        android:autofillHints="emailAddress"
                        android:hint="@string/register_email_hint"
                        android:imeOptions="actionNext"
                        android:inputType="textEmailAddress" />
                </com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout>

                <TextView
                    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.FieldLabel"
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:labelFor="@id/phoneInput"
                    android:text="@string/register_phone_label" />

                <!--
                    The phone number: a country selector and the local number beside it.

                    **Why two boxes rather than one.** A number only means anything against a country
                    — how many digits it has, which prefixes exist, what it looks like written
                    internationally — so asking for the country explicitly is what lets the form check
                    the number properly instead of guessing, and what lets it be stored in one
                    canonical international form. The behaviour behind the pair (the country chooser,
                    the length cap, the inline error) is ui/common/PhoneNumberInput.kt, shared with
                    every other phone field in the app.

                    A horizontal LinearLayout: the selector at a fixed width so the number box always
                    starts in the same place (see phone_country_field_width in dimens.xml), and the
                    number taking the rest with width 0 + weight 1.

                    baselineAligned="false" for the reason set out on the consent row below — a
                    baseline-aligning row measures a weighted child before it knows its width, which
                    with two text fields of different content leaves the row the wrong height.
                -->
                <LinearLayout
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:baselineAligned="false"
                    android:orientation="horizontal">

                    <!-- Not typed into: tapping it opens the country list. PhoneNumberInput makes it
                         unfocusable, so it can never summon the keyboard, and gives it a spoken
                         description naming the country in full — the label itself is a flag and a
                         number, which a screen reader cannot usefully read out. -->
                    <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout
                        android:id="@+id/phoneCountryLayout"
                        style="@style/PetCare.Auth.Field"
                        android:layout_width="@dimen/phone_country_field_width"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content">

                        <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputEditText
                            android:id="@+id/phoneCountryInput"
                            style="@style/PetCare.Auth.FieldInput"
                            android:layout_width="match_parent"
                            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                            android:inputType="none"
                            tools:text="🇳🇵 +977" />
                    </com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout>

                    <!-- inputType="phone" brings up the dial-pad keyboard. The number of digits it
                         will accept is capped per country by PhoneNumberInput, so an over-long number
                         cannot be typed at all. -->
                    <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout
                        android:id="@+id/phoneLayout"
                        style="@style/PetCare.Auth.Field"
                        android:layout_width="0dp"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                        android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/phone_country_field_gap"
                        android:layout_weight="1">

                        <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputEditText
                            android:id="@+id/phoneInput"
                            style="@style/PetCare.Auth.FieldInput"
                            android:layout_width="match_parent"
                            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                            android:autofillHints="phone"
                            android:hint="@string/register_phone_hint"
                            android:imeOptions="actionNext"
                            android:inputType="phone" />
                    </com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout>

                </LinearLayout>

                <!-- A standalone helper line rather than TextInputLayout's helperText, because the
                     mockup sets it in the mono helper face, and an error on the field should appear
                     *above* this note (as TextInputLayout's own error line does) rather than replace
                     it. -->
                <TextView
                    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.Helper"
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/auth_label_gap"
                    android:text="@string/register_phone_helper" />

                <TextView
                    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.FieldLabel"
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:labelFor="@id/registerPasswordInput"
                    android:text="@string/register_password_label" />

                <!-- The "At least 8 characters" placeholder is set by AuthFragment from the same
                     constant the validation uses. The eye icon works exactly as on the log in field
                     above, and independently of it and of the confirmation field below: each
                     TextInputLayout owns its own toggle and its own masked/revealed state.

                     The maximum length is applied by AuthFragment rather than written here, from the
                     same AuthValidation constant the rule uses — a number typed into the layout could
                     drift from the one enforced, and a field that accepted more than the form does
                     would refuse a paste only after the user pressed the button. -->
                <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout
                    android:id="@+id/registerPasswordLayout"
                    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.Field"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    app:endIconContentDescription="@string/cd_toggle_password_visibility"
                    app:endIconMode="password_toggle">

                    <!-- textPassword rather than textNewPassword: this field is revealed by the eye
                         icon beside it, and on some keyboards textNewPassword suppresses that in
                         favour of the keyboard's own suggestion flow. -->
                    <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputEditText
                        android:id="@+id/registerPasswordInput"
                        style="@style/PetCare.Auth.FieldInput"
                        android:layout_width="match_parent"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                        android:autofillHints="newPassword"
                        android:imeOptions="actionNext"
                        android:inputType="textPassword"
                        tools:hint="At least 8 characters" />
                </com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout>

                <!-- The six rules, ticking off live as the password is typed. Directly under the
                     field it describes and above the confirmation, which is where someone inventing a
                     password is already looking. See ui/common/PasswordChecklistView.kt for why the
                     rules are shown while the password is being chosen rather than reported after it
                     has been rejected. -->
                <com.prash.petcare.ui.common.PasswordChecklistView
                    android:id="@+id/registerPasswordChecklist"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/auth_label_gap" />

                <TextView
                    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.FieldLabel"
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:labelFor="@id/confirmPasswordInput"
                    android:text="@string/register_confirm_password_label" />

                <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout
                    android:id="@+id/confirmPasswordLayout"
                    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.Field"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    app:endIconContentDescription="@string/cd_toggle_password_visibility"
                    app:endIconMode="password_toggle">

                    <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputEditText
                        android:id="@+id/confirmPasswordInput"
                        style="@style/PetCare.Auth.FieldInput"
                        android:layout_width="match_parent"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                        android:autofillHints="newPassword"
                        android:hint="@string/register_confirm_password_hint"
                        android:imeOptions="actionDone"
                        android:inputType="textPassword" />
                </com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout>

                <!--
                    The consent row: a checkbox and the sentence beside it.

                    The sentence is a separate TextView rather than the checkbox's own text because it
                    contains a link ("terms"). AuthFragment makes tapping the rest of the sentence
                    toggle the box, and tapping the link open the terms instead — see bindConsentText().

                    gravity="top" on the row keeps the box aligned with the first line when the
                    sentence wraps to two, as it does in the mockup. The checkbox's minimum size is
                    removed so it sits tight against the text; Material's checked colour is the
                    theme's yellow already.

                    **baselineAligned="false" is load-bearing, not tidiness.** A horizontal
                    LinearLayout that aligns its children on their baselines measures a *weighted*
                    child before it knows how wide that child will be, and then keeps the height from
                    that first measurement. The sentence below is weighted, so it was measured as one
                    line at full width, wrapped to two when it was actually laid out, and the row kept
                    the one-line height — leaving the second line to spill out under the row and
                    behind the "CREATE ACCOUNT" button. Switching baseline alignment off makes the row
                    take its height from the wrapped text, which is what lets it grow with a longer
                    translation or a larger font scale instead of overlapping.
                -->
                <LinearLayout
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_md"
                    android:baselineAligned="false"
                    android:gravity="top"
                    android:orientation="horizontal">

                    <com.google.android.material.checkbox.MaterialCheckBox
                        android:id="@+id/consentCheckBox"
                        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                        android:layout_marginStart="-6dp"
                        android:contentDescription="@string/register_consent"
                        android:minWidth="0dp"
                        android:minHeight="0dp" />

                    <TextView
                        android:id="@+id/consentText"
                        style="@style/PetCare.Auth.Consent"
                        android:layout_width="0dp"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                        android:layout_marginTop="6dp"
                        android:layout_weight="1"
                        tools:text="@string/register_consent" />
                </LinearLayout>

                <TextView
                    android:id="@+id/consentError"
                    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.ErrorText"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_xs"
                    android:visibility="gone"
                    tools:text="@string/error_consent_required"
                    tools:visibility="visible" />

                <!-- A clear gap above the button, so the consent sentence still reads as a separate
                     thing to agree to once it has wrapped to two or three lines. -->
                <com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton
                    android:id="@+id/registerButton"
                    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.PrimaryButton"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_lg"
                    android:text="@string/register_submit" />
            </LinearLayout>

            <!-- Shown only while a sign-in or sign-up is being checked. GONE takes no space, so the
                 layout does not jump when it appears. -->
            <com.google.android.material.progressindicator.LinearProgressIndicator
                android:id="@+id/progressIndicator"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_sm"
                android:indeterminate="true"
                android:visibility="gone"
                app:trackCornerRadius="2dp" />

            <!-- ============================ Shared footer ============================ -->

            <!-- "—— OR ——": two weighted hairlines with the label between them, so the lines stretch
                 to fill whatever width the label leaves. -->
            <LinearLayout
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_md"
                android:gravity="center_vertical"
                android:orientation="horizontal">

                <View style="@style/PetCare.Auth.DividerLine" />

                <TextView
                    style="@style/PetCare.Auth.DividerLabel"
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:text="@string/auth_or" />

                <View style="@style/PetCare.Auth.DividerLine" />
            </LinearLayout>

            <!-- "Continue with Google", shared by both forms. It opens Google's account picker through
                 Credential Manager (ui/auth/GoogleSignInLauncher.kt), and the account chosen there
                 signs in to Firebase. On the register form it needs the terms ticked, like "Create
                 account". It is disabled with the other buttons while a sign-in is running. -->
            <com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton
                android:id="@+id/googleButton"
                style="@style/PetCare.Auth.GoogleButton"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_md"
                android:text="@string/auth_continue_with_google" />

            <!-- The way to the other form, at the very foot of the page: "New to PetCare? Create an
                 account" on log in, and "Already have an account? Log in" on register. The sentence is
                 one TextView with only its last words tappable — AuthFragment attaches the link to the
                 range marked by an <annotation> in the string, the same technique as the consent
                 sentence above, so a translation can place the link wherever its grammar needs it. -->
            <TextView
                android:id="@+id/switchFormText"
                style="@style/PetCare.Auth.SwitchFormText"
                android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_lg"
                tools:text="@string/login_register_prompt" />

        </LinearLayout>

        <!-- The locked screen, in place of everything above. Starts GONE and takes no space; see the
             note at the top of view_auth_locked.xml. -->
        <include
            android:id="@+id/lockedPanel"
            layout="@layout/view_auth_locked"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:visibility="gone" />

    </LinearLayout>
</ScrollView>
